+# BUG-20261015-379: Native repository Hook consent and root discovery
+
+Status: implementation and verification in progress. No completed fix is claimed yet.
+
+## Scope and observed behavior
+
+The native host Hook loader accepts an exact canonical working-directory grant. A Session opened below a repository root cannot explicitly authorize that repository's root Hook configuration through the native Hook interface. An existing exact root grant must not silently become a repository-wide grant.
+
+Pinned reference source selects the discovered Git root at Session startup and reload, and exposes explicit repository trust and untrust actions. The installed reference's trusted-root comparison additionally ran nested Hook files; that difference is retained rather than presented as identical behavior. The bounded native slice implements explicit repository consent and root discovery, preserving existing exact-folder grants. Administrator-enforced or signed Hook policy is a separate scope.
+
+## Expected behavior
+
+The operator can inspect the canonical repository root, explicitly allow its Hooks, and reload a captured Session at the existing safe boundary. Trust never moves the Session or changes filesystem, sandbox, MCP, LSP or plugin permissions. A nested repository remains a separate authority. Revocation blocks new command and HTTP Hook work from captured sources across Sessions; regrant requires fresh capture or explicit reload.
+
+## Implementation boundaries under verification
+
+A versioned host-data trust store binds grants to canonical root and Git metadata identity. Native Git discovery remains authoritative. Source construction attaches immutable authority proof; configuration fields cannot forge it. Store changes serialize with the existing lock mechanism and recheck Session incarnation and captured owner before publication. Reads are bounded and verify descriptor, path and parent identity, including final size and modification time. Symlinks and unknown metadata errors fail closed. Temporary cleanup requires the original inode and unchanged parent.
+
+The dispatch fence checks current host consent without reloading executable definitions. HTTPS repeats that check after DNS, CA and transport preparation, immediately before the request. Existing exact grants keep their scope; an explicit native repository denial restricts those sources within that repository without rewriting the legacy store. A legacy explicit denial remains restrictive. The UI distinguishes a saved grant from whether it is currently allowed.
+
+## Reproduction and verification plan
+
+Run the focused repository Hook test from `packages/apexo`, alongside existing Hook source, snapshot, observer cancellation and preference tests. Exercise source CLI and the rebuilt Desktop backend with the repository trust acceptance script. Run the real App/TUI consent and revoke surfaces, including canonical translated labels. Generate Client and legacy SDK outputs before affected package typechecks and HTTP API exercises.
+
+Required controls include root discovery only after explicit consent, no broadening of legacy exact grants, stale preview rejection, nested repository boundaries, canonical aliases, changed Git/root metadata, malformed and symlinked stores, concurrent updates, queued owner retirement, real command/HTTPS revocation, and restart persistence. Negative and positive results will be recorded after the coordinated runtime slot becomes available.
+
+No recurring-rule promotion is justified by this new capability gap or by multiple review comments on the same draft.
